hard disk sector sizes, and why a disk that works under one system
is unreadable under the other

src/hwsim/DISKS


the two controllers are not equally flexible
-------------------------------------------

HDCA has one sector size and no way to ask for another.  hdca.c says

    #define SECLEN  512     // fixed sector size

and uses it unconditionally - select_drive calls drive_sectorsize with
it, and both the read and the write path check the transfer against it.
512 is baked deeper in than the one constant, too: the read case shuffles
the sector through the controller's buffer as

    bcopy(&iobuf[0], &buffer[2], 510);
    bcopy(&iobuf[510], &buffer[0], 2);

because the last two bytes of a sector land at the start of the window.
Those 510s and 2s are the sector size written out longhand, so making
HDCA take another size is more than changing SECLEN.

FORMATHD offers no sector size choice either; its menu is only format a
logical drive, format a physical drive, continue, and diagnose.

HDDMA is programmable.  The size comes from the format or specify
channel command (the FSECSIZE and SSECSIZE macros in hddma.c decode it),
lib/harddisk.c records it in the drive label, and every later transfer
uses the recorded value.  FORMATMW takes "size <n>" with 128, 256, 512,
1024 or 2048 allowed, and defaults to 1024.


the sensitivity
---------------

Nothing in the simulator ties the sector size a disk was formatted with
to the sector size the operating system expects, because nothing in the
hardware did either.  A drive holds whatever the format program wrote,
and a BIOS asks for whatever it was built for.  When they disagree the
result is not an error - it is silent corruption, because every transfer
is the wrong length and lands at the wrong offset.

The two systems disagree by default:

    CP/M      wants the drive type's native size.  For an st506 that is
              1024 - the size FORMATMW uses when not told otherwise.

    Micronix  requires 512.  FORMATMW.DOC is explicit: "Users of the
              Morrow Designs multi-user operating system, Micronix, are
              required to format their disks with 512 byte sectors."

So a drive prepared for Micronix cannot be read by CP/M and a drive
prepared for CP/M cannot be read by Micronix, on the same controller,
with the same format program.  Only the "size" argument differs.


what it looks like when it is wrong
-----------------------------------

Formatting an st506 with size 512 and then using it from CP/M:

    A>PIP E:=A:*.COM[V]
    COPYING -
    ASM.COM
    VERIFY ERROR: =A:*.COM[V]

and a file copied without [V] is written but comes back wrong - the
copy of STAT.COM ran and printed nothing.  On the drive image only the
first 4K block of a two block file had been written at all.

Two ways to see it without guessing:

  - Arithmetic.  STAT reported 5408k free.  153 cylinders * 4 heads *
    17 sectors * 512 is 5202K, less than CP/M believed it had; at 1024
    the geometry is 9 sectors per track and 153 * 4 * 9 * 1024 is 5508K,
    which is the "5,508 1K sectors" FORMATMW.DOC lists for the st506.

  - The access trace.  lib/harddisk.c prints every cylinder/head/sector
    it touches, and the BIOS was walking sectors 0 through 8 - nine to a
    track, which is the 1024 byte geometry, not the seventeen the drive
    had been formatted with.

Reformatting with plain "FORMATMW st506" produced secsize 1024,
153 cylinders, 4 heads, 9 sectors, 5642240 bytes, and the same PIP with
verify copied 21 files with no errors.

Note that hddma.c does NOT adapt.  The line in the specify case that
would have adopted the requested size is commented out

    // secsz[drv] = secsize;

so a specify that disagrees with the label only prints

    drive 0 sectorsize mismatch on specify 512 expected 2048

and the transfer goes ahead at the label's size.  That is arguably the
honest behaviour - a real controller cannot reformat a platter because
the host asked it to - but it means the warning in the log is the only
notice you get before the corruption starts.


boot disks
----------

    922.IMD                    CP/M for HDDMA.  Banner reads
                               "AB: DJDMA 8", CD: DJDMA 5 1/4", EF: HDDMA".
                               Carries BOOTMW, FORMATMW, CBIOS.ASM and
                               MOVCPM5/MOVCPM10/MOVCPM16.

    910.IMD                    CP/M for HDCA.  Banner reads
                               "... EF: HDCA Fujitsu M10".  Carries
                               BOOTHD, FORMATHD, HDLOCK, CBIOS.ASM,
                               DEBLOCK.ASM, DISKDEF.LIB and NOTESHD.DOC.

    DRIVE_A.IMD                the Micronix installation disk, also
    1009-8_cold-boot.IMD       shipped as 1009.  Format programs and
                               boot loaders only - no CP/M drive letter
                               for the hard disk at all, so files cannot
                               be PIPed onto one from here.

    1010-8_stand-alone.IMD     Micronix standalone.  /etc/signon tells
    UX141_SA.IMD               you which install script to source.

Both CP/M system disks map the hard disk to drives E and F.


which controller, and which install script
------------------------------------------

Which controllers a kernel supports depends on the kernel, and it
changed.  con.c in this tree - which is 1.61, main.c prints the version -
has HDCA gone:

    struct biovec biosw[] = {
        &nodev, &nulldev, &nulldev,         /* 0 = no device */
        &nodev, &nodev, &nodev,             /* 1 = HDCA - removed, obsolete */
        &djopen, &djclose, &djstrat,        /* 2 = DJ-DMA */
        &mwopen, &mwclose, &mwstrat,        /* 3 = HD-DMA */
    };

but 1.4 still had it, and 1.6 is where it was retired.  Do not read the
table above as describing every kernel in kernels/.

The boot banner tells you which one you are running, because main.c
prints only the devices that have a strategy routine:

    pr("disks: ");
    for (i = 1; i < nbdev; i++)
        if (biosw[i].strat != &nodev)
            pr("%s ", devname[i]);

so a 1.4 kernel says

    disks: hdca(rev4)  djdma  hddma

and a 1.61 kernel, with HDCA nodev, says only djdma and hddma.  If
hdca(rev4) is in the list the kernel can use it.

So on 1.4 all four install scripts are live, and the standalone signon
is the guide - it is also the one that gets the controllers the right
way round, where DRIVE_A's README has them swapped:

    m5init, m10init, m16init    HDDMA
    hdinit                      HDCA   (1.4 only; gone by 1.6)


and why the mismatch is an HDDMA problem only
---------------------------------------------

HDCA cannot get this wrong.  It has one sector size, it is 512, and 512
is what Micronix wants - so a drive FORMATHD prepared is already the
size the kernel expects, and there is no argument to get wrong.

The hazard belongs entirely to HDDMA, and it is the price of that
controller being the programmable one.  FORMATMW will happily give you
any of five sector sizes, only one of which Micronix can read, and the
default is not it.


where the drive files live
--------------------------

Controllers name units hddma-0..3 and hdca-0..3.  drive_open resolves
those in the directory given by -d, or the current one if there is no
-d.  It opens O_CREAT, so a name it cannot find is created blank rather
than refused: if a run seems to have lost its disk, check -d before
looking anywhere else.

